A revoke that takes its caller's process or table goes on, and stops for an interrupt as any other does,
but the caller cannot make the call again,
so where the interrupt lands decides how far the revoke gets, and the host and QEMU part ways;
a stopped call leaves its caller running and holding its table.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: a call stopped where its caller cannot make it again

diff --git a/kernel/cap.c b/kernel/cap.c
--- a/kernel/cap.c
+++ b/kernel/cap.c
@@ -254,7 +254,7 @@ bool cap_revoke_below(struct cap *root, const struct cap *through, bool preempt)
          * or the capability it was made through ends there:
          * the caller could not make the call again, so an interrupt must not decide how far it got.
          */
-        if (thread_table(core_self()->current) == NULL || through->type == CAP_NONE) {
+        if ((thread_table(core_self()->current) == NULL && 0) || through->type == CAP_NONE) {
             return true;
         }
         if (root->child != 0 && cap_stop_here(preempt)) {
